To perform an OWASP automation test, you need to integrate Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) or Static Application Security Testing (SAST) tools directly into your CI/CD pipeline using the OWASP Automation Framework.
The industry standard for this process is OWASP ZAP (Zed Attack Proxy), a powerful, open-source tool built specifically for automated vulnerability scanning.

Step 1: Select Your Core Automation Tools

Depending on your application type, choose the appropriate automated scanner:
• OWASP ZAP (DAST): Best for traditional web apps and REST APIs. It simulates active attacks on running environments.
• OWASP Dependency-Check (SCA): Automatically detects publicly disclosed vulnerabilities within your project’s third-party dependencies.
• SonarQube / Semgrep (SAST): Scans your source code for OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities before compilation.

Step 2: Define the Test Scope via the ZAP Automation Framework

Instead of using a manual GUI, utilize ZAP's Automation Framework (AF). This allows you to define your entire security test in a single, version-controlled YAML configuration file.
Create a zap.yaml file outlining the environment and the scan jobs:
yaml
env:
  contexts:
    - name: "WebApp-Context"
      urls:
        - "https://your-staging-app.com"
      includePaths:
        - "https://your-staging-app.com.*"
      authentication:
        # Configure login scripts or token verification here if needed
jobs:
  - type: passiveScan-config
    parameters:
      maxAlertsPerRule: 10
  - type: spider
    parameters:
      context: "WebApp-Context"
  - type: activeScan
    parameters:
      context: "WebApp-Context"
  - type: report
    parameters:
      template: modern
      reportDir: "/workspace/reports"
      reportFile: "owasp-security-report"
